## What happened

In .NET 11, **DSA (Digital Signature Algorithm) was removed from macOS**. Calling `DSA.Create()` now throws a `PlatformNotSupportedException` on macOS. Windows and Linux are unaffected.

## How to fix

Since you only need to **verify** legacy DSA signatures (not create new ones), you have two options:

### Option 1: Platform-guard DSA verification (keep legacy support on Win/Linux)

```csharp
if (OperatingSystem.IsMacOS())
    throw new PlatformNotSupportedException("DSA verification not supported on macOS in .NET 11+");

using var dsa = DSA.Create();
dsa.ImportParameters(legacyPublicKey);
bool valid = dsa.VerifyData(data, signature, HashAlgorithmName.SHA1);
```

### Option 2: Migrate to ECDSA or RSA (recommended long-term)

Re-sign or re-issue with a modern algorithm and update verification:

```csharp
using var ecdsa = ECDsa.Create();
ecdsa.ImportSubjectPublicKeyInfo(publicKeyBytes, out _);
bool valid = ecdsa.VerifyData(data, signature, HashAlgorithmName.SHA256);
```

If you must verify existing DSA signatures on macOS, you'd need a third-party crypto library (e.g., BouncyCastle) since the platform no longer provides DSA support.
